<!-- Analysis -->
<div class="analysis">
  <h3>Analysis — why the regression</h3>
  <div class="analysis-grid">
    <div class="analysis-item">
      <div class="ai-label">Tiled vs batched matmul</div>
      <p>
        The mega kernel performs QKV projection one <code>bq_tile</code> at a time (128 tokens per tile, 125 tiles for 16k tokens). Each tile is a <strong>[128 × 2560] × [2560 × 6144]</strong> matmul. The baseline issues a single <strong>[16000 × 2560] × [2560 × 6144]</strong> matmul — 125× larger, far better MXU utilization. Small-tile matmuls pay per-tile dispatch overhead and leave the MXU underutilized.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="analysis-item">
      <div class="ai-label">The trade-off doesn't hold at prefill scale</div>
      <p>
        Mega kernel saves <strong>~6.4ms</strong> in external fusion ops (QKV projections no longer sit outside the RPA kernel), but adds <strong>+32.1ms</strong> to the RPA kernel itself. At 16k-token prefill the full-batch matmul wins by a wide margin. The decode path is unaffected (<code>RPAd ≈ 1.0×</code>), consistent with there being only 1 token per sequence — tiling is not the bottleneck at decode time.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="analysis-item">
      <div class="ai-label">Extra overhead ops</div>
      <p>
        Mega kernel introduces <code>copy_bitcast_fusion</code> (72 calls, 1.34ms) and more <code>pad_maximum_fusion</code> (37 calls vs 1 call, 0.97ms) — these are likely the <code>x</code> HBM→VMEM DMA staging and padding checks per bq tile. Not a primary cost but contributes ~2ms per forward pass.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="analysis-item">
      <div class="ai-label">Decode is neutral</div>
      <p>
        Both runs show <code>RPAd ≈ 0.007ms/call</code>. With only 1 token per decode step, the QKV projection is trivially small in both paths — no advantage or disadvantage there. The mega_kernel benefit would be most visible at <strong>very small prefill batch sizes</strong> or when the projection weight load from HBM dominates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

